The '''AFA World Cup''', often called the '''World Cup''', is an international {{w|association football}} competition among the senior men's national teams of the members of the [[Adonian Football Association]] (AFA), the sport's global governing body. The tournament has been held every four years since the [[1958 AFA World Cup|inaugural tournament in 1958]].

The contest starts with the qualification phase, which takes place over the preceding three years to determine which teams qualify for the tournament phase. In the tournament phase, 128 teams compete for the title at venues within the host nation(s) over the course of about a month. The host nation(s) automatically qualify for the group stage of the tournament.

== History ==

The [[Adonian Football Association]] was formed in 1954 during the second [[Adonian Community]] legislative convention as part of an initiative to improve international cooperation through sport. The agency, which operates under the [[Ministry for Culture (Adonian Community)|Ministry for Culture]], operates as a semi-independent governance body.

The first World Cup was initially planned for 1956 in [[Ampuria]], [[Volisania]], but was rescheduled to 1958 as organising initial qualifiers of 86 initial nations had required additional time and coordination from the body.

=== Early tournaments (1958–1970) ===

The [[1958 AFA World Cup]], hosted by [[Volisania]], was won by [[Zong]], who defeated [[Balisca]] in the final. The tournament established the World Cup as the premier event in Adonian football and set the pattern of quadrennial competition that has continued to the present day.

[[Volisania]] won the [[1962 AFA World Cup|1962 tournament]] in [[Zong]], becoming the first nation to win a World Cup hosted by another country. [[Balisca]] won their first title at [[1966 AFA World Cup|1966]] in [[Daras]], while [[Asikyira]] claimed their first championship at [[1970 AFA World Cup|1970]] in [[Balisca]].

=== Expansion and competitive balance (1974–1994) ===

The [[United Midlands]] won the [[1974 AFA World Cup|1974 tournament]] in [[Jarin]], their first title. [[Galwa]] won their only World Cup at [[1978 AFA World Cup|1978]] in the United Midlands, becoming the sixth nation to claim the title. [[Balisca]] won their second title at [[1982 AFA World Cup|1982]] in [[Okaiken]].

[[Asikyira]] won their second title at the [[1986 AFA World Cup|1986 tournament]] in [[Burawa]], defeating the host nation 2–1 in the final.{{sfn|AFC|1987}} [[Volisania]] won their second title at [[1990 AFA World Cup|1990]] in [[Dakare]], and [[Balisca]] claimed their third championship at [[1994 AFA World Cup|1994]] in [[Balboa]].

=== Modern era (1998–present) ===

[[Asikyira]] won their third title at [[1998 AFA World Cup|1998]] in [[Nakamizu]]. [[Okaiken]] won their first World Cup at [[2002 AFA World Cup|2002]], co-hosted by [[Louyang]] and [[Sinchew]], becoming the first East Fosian nation to win the tournament.

The [[2006 AFA World Cup]] in [[Breisland]] was won by [[Balboa]], who defeated Asikyira 3–1 in the final after trailing at half-time.{{sfn|AFC|2007}} The match, defined by the performance of [[Nèstir Cavall]], is widely regarded as the greatest comeback in World Cup history and the greatest individual display in AFA World Cup history.{{sfn|AFC|2007|p=84}} Balboa became the ninth and most recent nation to win the World Cup.

[[Burawa]] won their only title at [[2010 AFA World Cup|2010]] in [[Nusaraya]]. The [[United Midlands]] won their second title at [[2014 AFA World Cup|2014]] in [[Varkana]]. [[Asikyira]] won their fourth title at [[2018 AFA World Cup|2018]] in [[Ayorin]], and [[Balisca]] claimed their fourth championship at [[2022 AFA World Cup|2022]] in [[Shansi]].

The [[2026 AFA World Cup]] is scheduled to be co-hosted by [[Balisca]] and [[Sanrobonia]], and the [[2030 AFA World Cup]] is scheduled for [[Dagit]].

== Format ==

Since the second World Cup in 1962, qualifying tournaments have been held to thin the field for the final tournament. They are held within the seven AFA regions (Abyala, Lurandia, Mesogean, North and Central Kaftia, East Fosia, Central Fosia and Southern Illypnia, and North Fosia and Kendryian), overseen by their respective confederations. For each tournament, AFA decides the number of places awarded to each of the zones beforehand, generally based on the relative strength of the confederations' teams.

The qualification process can start as early as almost three years before the final tournament and last over a two-year period. The formats of the qualification tournaments differ between confederations. Usually, one or two places are awarded to winners of interregional play-offs.

=== Final tournament ===

The final tournament features 32 national teams that compete over the course of a month in the host nations. There are two stages: the group stage, followed by the knockout stage.

In the group stage, teams compete within eight groups of four teams each. Eight teams are seeded, including the hosts, with the other seeded teams selected using a formula based on the AFA World Rankings or performances in recent World Cups, and drawn to separate groups. The other teams are assigned to different "pots", usually based on geographical criteria, and teams in each pot are drawn at random to the eight groups.

Each group plays a round-robin tournament in which each team is scheduled for three matches against other teams in the same group. The top two teams from each group advance to the knockout stage. Points are used to rank the teams within a group: three points for a win, one for a draw, and none for a loss.

The knockout stage is a {{w|single-elimination tournament}} in which teams play each other in one-off matches, with {{w|extra time}} and {{w|penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shootouts}} used to decide the winner if necessary. It begins with the round of 16, followed by the quarter-finals, the semi-finals, the {{w|third-place play-off|match for third place}}, and the final.

